{"date": "2026-10-21", "post_id": 1495620, "title": "New Ghosts Revealed in Cassiopeia", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-21-new-ghosts-revealed-in-cassiopeia/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> Jinkies, look at all those ghosts! It took 100 hours of observation and a special oxygen <a href=\"https://www.schoolsobservatory.org/learn/telescopes/how/filters\">filter</a> for <a href=\"https://app.astrobin.com/i/6b6z2r\">today\u2019s image</a> to capture three faint <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/universe/stories/quick-reads/decoding-nebulae/\">nebulas</a> hidden in the <a href=\"https://noirlab.edu/public/education/constellations/cassiopeia/\">Cassiopeia constellation</a>. IC 59 and <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/asset/hubble/ic-63-ghost-nebula/\">IC 63</a>, the original <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-october-31-ghosts-in-cassiopeia/\">Ghosts of Cassiopeia</a>, haunt the region near multi-star system <a href=\"https://archive.aavso.org/vsots_gammacas\">\u03b3 Cassiopeia</a>. <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/ems/10_ultravioletwaves/\">Energetic light</a> from \u03b3 Cassiopeia causes the hydrogen gas in IC 59 and 63 to glow red. Their <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/universe/stories/quick-reads/be-glad-you-dont-have-to-dust-in-space/\">dust</a> reflects and scatters some blue-ish white starlight. Newly discovered Objects 1, 2, and 3 appear as blue phantoms, revealing the presence of ionized oxygen. But ionized by what? Like at the bow of a speeding boat, \u03b3 Cassiopeia may be piling up material as the system moves through interstellar gas and dust. It follows that Object 3 may be the compressed and heated oxygen of that <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/science-research/astrophysics/cosmic-bow-shocks/\">bow shock</a>. Object 1 could be a <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-june-22-m27-the-dumbbell-nebula/\">planetary nebula</a> if a central <a href=\"https://imagine.gsfc.nasa.gov/science/objects/dwarfs1.html\">white dwarf</a> is identified. The origins of these astronomical apparitions remain a mystery to be solved.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> it's not easy being green", "credit": "<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/lrx.photo/\">Louis Leroux-G\u00e9r\u00e9</a>,\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/tybr\">Thibault Rouill\u00e9e</a>,\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/vincent.astro/\">Vincent Martin</a>,\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/lesphotosdecedric/\">C\u00e9dric de Decker</a> (<a href=\"https://starfront.space/\">Starfront Observatories</a>)", "copyright": "<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/lrx.photo/\">Louis Leroux-G\u00e9r\u00e9</a>,\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/tybr\">Thibault Rouill\u00e9e</a>,\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/vincent.astro/\">Vincent Martin</a>,\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/lesphotosdecedric/\">C\u00e9dric de Decker</a> (<a href=\"https://starfront.space/\">Starfront Observatories</a>)", "alt": "The sky is speckled with stars. Bright tufts of emission nebulae trace across the image&#039;s equator. Arcs and smudges of ionized oxygen emission also included.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-21-new-ghosts-revealed-in-cassiopeia/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/IC63.png?w=2622&h=1751&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 October 21 - New Ghosts Revealed in Cassiopeia</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-21-new-ghosts-revealed-in-cassiopeia/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: Jinkies, look at all those ghosts! It took 100 hours of observation and a special oxygen filter for today\u2019s image to capture three faint nebulas hidden in the Cassiopeia constellation. IC 59 and IC 63, the original Ghosts of Cassiopeia, haunt the region near multi-star system \u03b3 Cassiopeia. Energetic light from \u03b3 Cassiopeia causes the hydrogen gas in IC 59 and 63 to glow red. Their dust reflects and scatters some blue-ish white starlight. Newly discovered Objects 1, 2, and 3 appear as blue phantoms, revealing the presence of ionized oxygen. But ionized by what? Like at the bow of a speeding boat, \u03b3 Cassiopeia may be piling up material as the system moves through interstellar gas and dust. It follows that Object 3 may be the compressed and heated oxygen of that bow shock. Object 1 could be a planetary nebula if a central white dwarf is identified. The origins of these astronomical apparitions remain a mystery to be solved.Tomorrow&#039;s picture: it&#039;s not easy being green\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"new ghosts revealed in cassiopeia\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"New Ghosts Revealed in Cassiopeia\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-21-new-ghosts-revealed-in-cassiopeia/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/IC63.png?w=2622&#038;h=1751&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/IC63.png?w=2622&#038;h=1751&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"The sky is speckled with stars. Bright tufts of emission nebulae trace across the image&#039;s equator. Arcs and smudges of ionized oxygen emission also included.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-10-21T00:05:00-04:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-10-20T14:55:24-04:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"New Ghosts Revealed in Cassiopeia\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/IC63.png?w=2622&#038;h=1751&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 October 21<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/IC63.png\" onMouseOver=\"if (document.images) document.imagename1.src='https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/IC63_annoted.png';\"\nonMouseOut=\"if (document.images) document.imagename1.src='https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/IC63.png';\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/IC63.png\" name=imagename1 alt=\"The sky is speckled with stars. Bright tufts of emission nebulae trace across the image&#039;s equator. Arcs and smudges of ionized oxygen emission also included.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> New Ghosts Revealed in Cassiopeia </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/lrx.photo/\">Louis Leroux-G\u00e9r\u00e9</a>,\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/tybr\">Thibault Rouill\u00e9e</a>,\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/vincent.astro/\">Vincent Martin</a>,\u00a0<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/lesphotosdecedric/\">C\u00e9dric de Decker</a> (<a href=\"https://starfront.space/\">Starfront Observatories</a>)\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> Jinkies, look at all those ghosts! It took 100 hours of observation and a special oxygen <a href=\"https://www.schoolsobservatory.org/learn/telescopes/how/filters\">filter</a> for <a href=\"https://app.astrobin.com/i/6b6z2r\">today\u2019s image</a> to capture three faint <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/universe/stories/quick-reads/decoding-nebulae/\">nebulas</a> hidden in the <a href=\"https://noirlab.edu/public/education/constellations/cassiopeia/\">Cassiopeia constellation</a>. IC 59 and <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/asset/hubble/ic-63-ghost-nebula/\">IC 63</a>, the original <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-october-31-ghosts-in-cassiopeia/\">Ghosts of Cassiopeia</a>, haunt the region near multi-star system <a href=\"https://archive.aavso.org/vsots_gammacas\">\u03b3 Cassiopeia</a>. <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/ems/10_ultravioletwaves/\">Energetic light</a> from \u03b3 Cassiopeia causes the hydrogen gas in IC 59 and 63 to glow red. Their <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/universe/stories/quick-reads/be-glad-you-dont-have-to-dust-in-space/\">dust</a> reflects and scatters some blue-ish white starlight. Newly discovered Objects 1, 2, and 3 appear as blue phantoms, revealing the presence of ionized oxygen. But ionized by what? Like at the bow of a speeding boat, \u03b3 Cassiopeia may be piling up material as the system moves through interstellar gas and dust. It follows that Object 3 may be the compressed and heated oxygen of that <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/science-research/astrophysics/cosmic-bow-shocks/\">bow shock</a>. Object 1 could be a <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-june-22-m27-the-dumbbell-nebula/\">planetary nebula</a> if a central <a href=\"https://imagine.gsfc.nasa.gov/science/objects/dwarfs1.html\">white dwarf</a> is identified. The origins of these astronomical apparitions remain a mystery to be solved.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> it's not easy being green\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-20-an-anticrepuscular-rainbow-fan/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nKeighley Rockcliffe, Robert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261021/html"}